# Identity & Mission

I am a Senior Software Engineer with 15+ years of experience building modern, scalable software systems.

This project is a portfolio-grade, enterprise-style, multi-tenant SaaS e-commerce platform that serves as a long-term technical demonstration of modern software architecture, engineering practices, and product development.

The Goal:

Build a production-grade, multi-tenant SaaS platform that demonstrates:

- Domain-Driven Design (DDD)
- Vertical Slice Architecture
- Modular architecture with bounded contexts
- Event-driven design and asynchronous workflows
- Multi-tenancy and data isolation
- Clean Architecture principles
- Modern .NET backend development
- Angular enterprise application development
- Next.js marketplace architecture
- Cloud-native deployment, observability, and operational excellence

The system should be designed for evolutionary architecture, allowing modules to remain within a modular monolith or be extracted into independently deployable services when justified by business, operational, or scaling requirements.

Core Requirement:
Every line of code must reflect production-grade engineering quality. Prioritize maintainability, explicit design, type safety, testability, observability, and strict architectural boundaries over shortcuts or quick implementations.

This project serves as a long-term demonstration of how a modern SaaS platform can be designed, built, tested, deployed, operated, and evolved using production-grade engineering practices.

# AI Behavior Rules

- Always prioritize maintainability over brevity
- Do not generate placeholder or pseudo code
- If uncertain, ask for clarification instead of guessing
- Always follow existing patterns in the codebase
- Do not introduce new libraries without justification
- Prefer explicit over implicit implementation
- Ensure all generated code compiles and is production-ready

# Rule Priority (Highest -> Lowest)

1. Business Rules Document
2. ADR Documents
3. Architecture & Dependency Rules
4. Coding Standards
5. Style / Formatting

If conflicts occur, follow this order strictly.

# The Tech Stack

Backend:

- Runtime: .NET 10 (latest)
- Framework: ASP.NET Core WEB API
- Persistence: PostgreSQL with EF Core (Neon.tech for Cloud / Local Docker)
- Auth: Clerk (Identity Provider) + Custom JWT Claims Transformation
- Testing: xUnit + FluentAssertions (Tests organized per Vertical Slice)
- Tooling: Central Package Management (Directory.Packages.Props), .slnx solution format

Frontend (Monorepo):

- Admin CMS: Angular v21 (Typescript)
  - State: Signals + Tanstack Query
  - Validation: Zod
  - UI: Angular Material (Enterprise standard) + TailwindCSS
  - Architecture: Feature-package approach with DDAU (Data Down, Actions Up)

- Marketplace: Next.js 16 (App Router - Typescript)
  - Components: React Server Components (RSC)
  - Validation: Zod
  - UI: shadcn/ui + TailwindCSS
  - Data: Server Actions + Module Pattern

- Testing: Vitest (Test files co-located with implementations)

Package Management:

- Frontend: pnpm (Workspace-aware, strict dependency hoisting)
- Backend: Central Package Management (Directory.Packages.Props)

Infrastructure & CI/CD:

- Automated PR checks (Linting, Build, Vitest, xUnit)
- Dockerization of backend and frontend apps
- Automated push to Github Packages (GHCR)
- Deployment to Railway (App Hosting) and Neon (DB Migrations)

- Migration Strategy: EF Core Migrations. Note: Rollbacks are handled via "Roll-Forward" migrations or manual DB snapshots in Neon to maintain integrity.

# Scalability Principles:

- Prefer read/write separation at the slice level when needed
- Design aggregates to minimize cross-tenant locking
- Use pagination and filtering for all list endpoints
- Avoid N+1 queries via Explicit Projections
- Prefer async streaming for large datasets
- Ensure all endpoints are safe under concurrent tenant access

# Architecture & Rules

- Pattern: Vertical Slice Architecture combined with DDD Aggregates
- Error Handling: Result Pattern (ADR-0004)
  - Failures are first-class data
  - All operations must return Result<T> or Result
  - Errors must be categorized:
    - Domain Errors (business rule violations)
    - Validation Errors (input issues)
    - Infrastructure Errors (external/system failures)
  - API layer must map errors to RFC 7807 ProblemDetails
  - Exceptions are only allowed for truly unexpected failures
- Isolation: Multi-tenancy via X-Tenant-Id and EF Global Query Filters (ADR-0001)
- Modeling: Explicit split between StoreBrand (Global) and StoreInstance (Tenant-scoped) (ADR-0002)
- Logic & Flow: Refer to ADR-0005 for naming conventions and layer responsibilities.
  - Treat each bounded context as a module with explicit public contracts and project boundaries.
  - Communicate across modules only via defined interfaces, DTO contracts, or integration events.

# Forbidden Practices

- No direct DbContext usage outside Infrastructure
- No module access to another module’s internal implementation, repository, or DbContext
- No shared kernel domain objects: shared libraries are for technical abstractions only, not business entities or domain logic
- No cross-slice dependencies
- No shared mutable state between tenants
- No bypassing Result Pattern with exceptions (except truly exceptional cases)
- No logic inside Controllers beyond orchestration
- No leaking Infrastructure concerns into Application layer

# Domain Model & Entities

Refer to ADR-0001 (Multi-Tenant Model) and ADR-0002 (Store Split) for the definitive entity relationships.

- Hierarchy: All entities must strictly follow the Tenant vs StoreBrand vs StoreInstance definitions found in context documents
- Implementation: Use TenantId as the discriminator for all scoped entities to satisfy the Global Query Filter requirement

# ADR Index

- ADR-0001: Multi-Tenant Model
- ADR-0002: Split Store into StoreBrand and StoreInstance
- ADR-0003: Application Structure - Vertical Slice and DDD aggregates
- ADR-0004: Error Handling - Results Pattern and Functional Error
- ADR-0005: 0005-modular-monolith-bounded-context-modules

# Migration & Platform Alignment

- Use `docs/migration-playbook-from-layered-monolith-to-modular-monolith.md` as the core migration roadmap.
- Follow the playbook’s per-module phase template when moving domains into ADR-0005 bounded-context modules.
- Apply the event-driven maturity ladder: in-process publisher first, then outbox persistence, then optional external broker.
- Do not use integration events as a replacement for normal synchronous command/query boundaries.
- Use `docs/platform/README.md` for optional post-migration platform guidance.
- Platform docs such as `docs/platform/dotnet-aspire-orchestration.md` and `docs/platform/opentelemetry-observability.md` are optional extras after the modular monolith is stable.

# Business & Security Rules

- Refer to the Business Rules document for all logic governing:
  - Tenant Onboarding (Rules T-01 to T-04)
  - Membership & Roles (Rules M-01 to R-01)
  - Store Operations (Rules S-01 to S-05)
  - Tenant Resolution (Rules SR-01 to SR-03)
- Do not implement any logic that contradicts the rules established in these documents. If a conflict arises, the Business Rules document is the authoritative source

# Pipeline & Middleware

The request pipeline order is strictly enforced to ensure security and observability. Refer below as the gold standard for implementation:

1. Observability & Safety (Top of Pipeline)

- Serilog: Must be first to capture the entire lifecycle
- ExceptionHandler: Uses GlobalExceptionHandler to convert unhandled exceptions into RFC 7807 Problem Details

2. Security & Context (Middle of the Pipeline)

- Correlation ID: Injected early for cross-service tracing
- Authentication: Validates the Clerk JWT
  - Note: JwtSecurityTokenHandler.DefaultInboundClaimTypeMap.Clear() is mandatory to prevent claim renaming.
- Tenant Resolution: Extracts X-Tenant-Id. Must run after Auth to ensure the user is identified but before Authorization
- Log Enrichment: Add UserId and TenantId to the Serilog properties for every log entry in that request
- Authorization: Policy-based checks

3. Dependency Injection Rules

- Database: Configured via AddDatabaseOptions (PostgreSQL/Neon)
- Identity & Tenancy: Services must be registered via AddIdentity() and AddTenancy()
- Vertical Slices: Business logic is registered via AddApplicationServices()

4. Resilience & Protection

- Rate Limiting (Inbound): Positioned immediately after UseExceptionHandler
  - Use User.Identity.Name or IP Address as the partition key
  - Return 429 Too Many Requests when limits are exceeded

- Resilience (Outbound): Implemented in the Infrastructure Layer using Polly
  - Event-driven integration should evolve along a safe ladder: in-process publisher -> outbox -> broker
  - Retry Patterns: 3 retries with exponential backoff for transient HTTP errors (5xx, 408)
  - Circuit Breaker: Open the circuit if failure rate > 50% over a 30s window to prevent cascading failures to external dependencies

5. Layered Dependency Injection

- Pattern: Each project layer (Application, Infrastructure, Api) must contain a DependencyInjection.cs file with an IServiceCollection extension method (e.g: .AddApplication(), .AddInfrastructure())
- Encapsulation:
  - The API project only calls the top-level extension method in Program.cs
  - Internal dependencies (like specific Repositories or Validators) should be registered within their respective layers
- Implementation: Use Primary Constructors for all injected dependencies in these classes

# Folder & Project Structure

The project is organized as a Monorepo. Root directory: uic-ecommerce/

1. Root Level

- Directory.Build.Props: Global MSBuild settings
- Directory.Packages.Props: Central Package Management (CPM)
- .cursorrules: Architectural instructions
- .cursorignore: Privacy and exclusion rules

2. Backend (/backend/ecommerce-api/)
   Follow Modular Monolith with Bounded Context Modules: ADR-0005: 0005-modular-monolith-bounded-context-modules

3. Apps (/apps/)

- admin/: Angular v21
  - src/: Application shell, main, config
  - libs/: Feature-package approach. Sub-folders: features/, ui/, core/

- marketplace/: Next.js 16
  - app/: Server router and pages
  - features/: Module pattern (Services/Repos/Actions)
  - shared/: ui/ (shadcn) and core/ (utils)

4. Infrastructure

- /docker/: Docker Compose and environment files
- Dockerfile: Multi-staged build co-located in the backend project root

- Optional platform guidance: see `docs/platform/README.md` for observability and orchestration patterns once ADR-0005 migration is stable.

# Coding Standards & Build Strategy

1. Testing Strategy (Frontend)

- Tooling: Use Vitest for both Angular and Next.js
- Location: Use Co-location. Test files (_.spec.ts or _.test.ts) must live in the same folder as the implementation
- Mocking: Use msw (Mock Service Worker) for API mocking to ensure tests are resilient to backend changes

2. Frontend Build & Configuration

- Typescript: Use a base TSConfig at the monorepo root. Each app (admin, marketplace) must have its own tsconfig.json that extends the root, with strict path aliases (e.g: @sharead/_, @features/_)
- Build Tooling: Use Vite as the underlying engine for Angular 21 and Vitest

3. Rendering & Hydration

- Next.js 16 (Marketplace):
  - Strategy: Default to Static Rendering for product catalogs (ISR/SSG) with a revalidation interval
  - Dynamic Parts: Use Partial Prerendering (PPR) for user-specific sections (cart, profile) to keep the shell fast

- Angular v21 (Admin):
  - Strategy: Use Client-Side Rendering (CSR) since it's a protected CMS
  - Hydration: Enable Zoneless Change Detection with Signals and Event Replay for maximum performance

4. C# Aesthetic

- Use Primary Constructors for DI in all layers
- Use Collection Expressions and var where the type is obvious
- Null Safety: Strict nullability enabled across all backend projects

5. ESLint (v9.39.2) - Flat Config

- Strictness: Enable @typecript-eslint/no-explicit-any: error
- Imports: Force consistent-type-imports with prefer: type-imports
- Order: use import/order with the following group sequence: builtin, external, internal/parent/sibling, type, then index/object
  - Alphabetize (ascending/case-insensitive)
  - New lines between groups
  - Treat @ecommerce/\*\* as an internal path group
- Rules:
  - Allow \_ prefix for unused variables (argsIgnorePattern)
  - Disallow console logs except warn and error
  - Enforce eqeqeq (strict equality) and curly (all braces)

6. Prettier

- Integration: Must run via eslint-plugin-prettier to avoid conflicts
- Rules: Single quotes, trailing commas (all), 2-space indentation, and 100 character print width

7. Backend Build & Dependency Management

- Central Package Management (CPM): Versions must live in Directory.Packages.Props only. No versions should be defined in individual .csproj files. This ensures a single version of EF Core, Serilog, or xUnit across the entire solution
- Global Props: Use Directory.Build.Props - enforce global settings:
  - <Nullable>enable</Nullable> and <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  - <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel> and <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild> to treat style warnings as build errors
  - <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors> for Release builds

8. EditorConfig & Formatting

- The .editorconfig acts as the "source of truth" for the IDE's behavior. It should enforce:
  - Root: root = true
  - General: charset = utf-8, indent_style = space, indent_size = 2 (Frontend) / 4 (Backend)
- C# Specifics:
  - Enforce File-scoped namespaces, Primary Constructors, and Remove unused usings on build
  - csharp_style_primary_constructors = true:suggestion
  - csharp_style_expression_bodied_methods = true:suggestion
  - csharp_style_namespace_declarations = file_scoped:error
  - dotnet_diagnostic.IDE0005.severity = error

# Backend Testing Strategy

1. Unit Tests:

- Domain logic
- Value objects
- Validators

2. Slice Tests:

- Test the primary use-case entry point of a vertical slice (e.g.: Application Services, Use-Case classes, or HandleAsync/ExecuteAsync methods)
- Do NOT assume CQRS or MediatR-style handlers unless explicitly defined
- Focus on business behavior, not framework patterns
- Use real implementations where possible (avoid excessive mocking)
- A slice represents a complete feature flow from entry point to persistence within architectural boundaries

3. Integration Tests:

- Full pipeline using WebApplicationFactory
- Validate middleware, auth, and tenant resolution

4. Database:

- Prefer real PostgreSQL (Testcontainers or Docker)
- Do NOT mock DbContext

5. Assertions:

- Use FluentAssertions for readability

# Definition of DONE

- Code compiles with zero warnings
- Tests exist and pass
- Follows architectural boundaries
- Includes validation and error handling
- Uses Result Pattern consistently
- Logging is included where relevant
- No architectural rule violations
- Code is understandable and explainable without additional context
